18:15:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target: This is a brilliant follow-up—it's the logical next step. If bacteria make amino acids, and we can absorb bacterial vitamins, why can't we just absorb enough amino acids from them? The answer involves **quantity**, **location**, and **competition**. ### 1. The Quantity Gap Is Enormous Vitamins are needed in **micrograms** (millionths of a gram) or **milligrams** per day. For example: - Vitamin K: ~120 mcg/day - Biotin: ~30 mcg/day Essential amino acids, however, are needed in **grams per day**: - A 70 kg adult needs about **~240 grams of protein** per day, part of which must be essential amino acids like lysine (~3 g/day), methionine (~1 g/day), etc. Total essential amino acid requirement: roughly **20–30 grams per day**. Gut bacteria produce amino acids primarily for their *own* growth and reproduction. Only a tiny fraction of that may leak into the lumen and be absorbed. Estimates suggest microbial amino acid contribution to the host is **less than 1–5%** of daily needs. If you rely on that, you'd develop severe protein deficiency within days. Vitamins, by contrast, are produced in concentrations that can meet a much larger fraction of the body's tiny need. ### 2. Location, Location, Location Most bacterial fermentation (including amino acid production) happens in the **large intestine (colon)**. The colon is primarily designed to absorb water, electrolytes, and some short-chain fatty acids—**not** amino acids. - The **small intestine** is where 90–95% of amino acid absorption occurs, thanks to specialized transporters. By the time food reaches the colon, nearly all amino acids from your diet have already been absorbed. - The colon has some amino acid transporters, but they are far less abundant and efficient. Amino acids that arrive in the colon (either from undigested protein or from bacteria) are mostly broken down further by bacteria into ammonia, hydrogen sulfide, and other gases—**not absorbed**. So even if bacteria produced large