*the quality and type of energy sources**, not just total calories or vitamin deficiencies. ### 3. The "dose-response" is clear - **High glycemic load** (even in normal-weight individuals) is associated with a **30–40% increased risk** of type 2 diabetes. - **High dietary advanced glycation end products** (from high-heat processed foods) are linked to increased oxidative stress, independent of calorie intake. - **High omega-6 to omega-3 ratio** from industrial seed oils is associated with higher inflammation and heart disease risk. These effects are not theoretical – they are consistent across hundreds of epidemiological studies and controlled trials. --- ## A useful analogy: The car engine | Requirement | Effect if missing | Effect of poor quality | |-------------|------------------|------------------------| | Enough fuel (energy) | Engine stops | – | | Essential lubricants (vitamins, minerals) | Engine seizes | – | | High-octane vs. low-octane fuel (macronutrient quality) | Engine still runs | Knocking, reduced efficiency, long-term damage | If you use low-octane fuel (refined carbs, bad fats), the engine may still run for years, but the cumulative damage shows up as **engine failure** (heart attack, diabetes, cancer) decades later. --- ## Quantitative estimate Based on meta-analyses: - **Getting enough energy and essential nutrients** prevents acute deficiency and allows basic function. This reduces disease risk by maybe **20–30%** compared to deficiency. - **Choosing high-quality energy sources** (whole grains, unsaturated fats, minimal added sugar, high fiber) reduces chronic disease risk by an additional **30–50%** compared to a nutrient-adequate but poor-quality diet. **So the source of energy is not trivial – it can double or triple the benefit you get from simply meeting your basic needs.** --- ## Bottom line: For a person who already eats enough calories and takes a multivitamin, switching from a diet of white bread, vegetable oil, and processed meat to on