t just cautious—it's ignoring the weight of the data. ### Conclusion You don't need absolute certainty to act. You need to weigh the consequences of being wrong. In this case, the consequences of assuming diet doesn't matter (if it does) are far worse than the consequences of assuming it does (if it doesn't). That is why, even with imperfect evidence, we should treat foods as different—because the risk of being wrong about "all foods are the same" is far too high.
